    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Dice Odds at a Glance</h2>
      <p>One die gives every face the same chance. Add more dice and the totals bunch up in the middle, which is why 7 is the number to watch in board games played with two six-sided dice.</p>
      <div class="table-wrapper">
        <table class="table">
          <caption class="visually-hidden">Ranges, averages and single-face chances for common dice</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Roll</th><th scope="col">Range</th><th scope="col">Average</th><th scope="col">Chance of one value</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>1d4</td><td>1–4</td><td>2.5</td><td>25%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>1d6</td><td>1–6</td><td>3.5</td><td>16.67%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2d6</td><td>2–12</td><td>7</td><td>7: 16.67%, 2 or 12: 2.78%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>1d8</td><td>1–8</td><td>4.5</td><td>12.5%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>1d10</td><td>1–10</td><td>5.5</td><td>10%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>1d12</td><td>1–12</td><td>6.5</td><td>8.33%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>1d20</td><td>1–20</td><td>10.5</td><td>5%</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>When to Flip a Coin or Roll a Die Online</h2>
      <ul class="content-list">
        <li><strong>Settling a decision:</strong> a coin flip is the classic fair tie-breaker between two options.</li>
        <li><strong>Board games and role-playing games:</strong> replace missing dice, or roll handfuls of d6 or a d20 quickly with the total worked out.</li>
        <li><strong>Teaching probability:</strong> flip 100 coins several times to show how the heads count varies, or compare 2d6 totals with the odds table above.</li>
        <li><strong>Random choices:</strong> for a choice among more than six options, use the <a href="/tools/random-number-generator/">random number generator</a> or the <a href="/tools/random-name-picker/">random name picker</a>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Physical coins and dice can be slightly biased by their shape and how they are thrown. A secure software generator does not have that problem: every outcome is exactly equally likely. Results are generated in your browser and the history stays on this page only.</p>
    </section>
